This is for training your own stem separation model from scratch. The idea is you take a bunch of songs, run them through the best existing models to get stems, then train a smaller model on that output. It's basically distillation but you pre-compute everything so training is fast.

The model is a band-split transformer - it chops the spectrogram into frequency bands, runs a transformer on each one, then reconstructs the stems. Nothing fancy, just the parts that work.

You'll want a cloud GPU for this (A100 on Lambda/RunPod, ~$1.50/hr). The whole thing takes maybe $10-15 and an afternoon.

Steps:
  1. Rent a GPU box, upload your music
  2. python3 batch_separate.py /path/to/music /path/to/stems
     (this runs htdemucs_6s on everything, takes 1-2hrs on GPU)
  3. python3 train.py --data /path/to/stems --size small --epochs 200
     (actual training, 4-8hrs)
  4. python3 inference.py checkpoints/best.pt song.wav output/
     (test it on something)
  5. Download best.pt (~50MB), use it in stem

Model sizes:
  tiny   ~3M params   probably sounds rough but runs in <1s
  small  ~8M params   good starting point
  medium ~15M params  if small isn't cutting it

The trained model runs on any machine - you train on a cloud A100 but inference works fine on CPU. The whole point is to get something small enough to run fast on ARM.
